  "query_preview": "from Wisconsin and Michigan, northwest to northern \nMinnesota. With the flow becoming anticyclonic lake effect rain \nshowers had largely diminished though a few sprinkles were still \nover the far eastern U.P. Surface observations showed seasonable \ntemperatures in the 40s to low 50s along with northwest winds around \n10 mph. It was a bit breezier over the Keweenaw and Lake Superior \nshoreline where gusts to 20 mph were occurring. \n\nTonight high pressure will strengthen overhead and the pressure \ngradient will weaken. This, coupled with boundary layer decoupling \nshould bring a cool night to much of the U.P. with lows falling into \nthe 20s to low 30s. Even areas near the shorelines will be colder as \na land breeze helps limit the influence of the warmer waters. \n\nUpper level ridging will build over the Upper Great Lakes for the \nweekend with a warming trend expected along with a push of Gulf \nmoisture. As the warmer, more moist air pushes into the area \nisentropic lift will bring increasing cloud cover Saturday afternoon \nalong with a period of scattered showers Saturday evening and \novernight. Low clouds and perhaps some advection fog could linger \ninto Sunday morning, potentially into the early afternoon. It won't \nfeel very much like November on Sunday into early next week. \nOvernight temperatures Saturday and Sunday night will be well above \naverage with lows in the mid 40s (close to the average high \ntemperature for the start of November). Highs will be in the 50s to \nlow 60s.\n\nMonday into Wednesday the West Coast trough opens up and moves east \nwith a surface low developing in the lee of the Central Rockies, \nthen tracking northeast to the Plains, and eventually into Ontario \nTuesday night. This track would bring a warm front north through the \narea Monday night with warm air advection resulting in a period of \nwidespread rainfall. Additional shower chances will continue into \nTuesday night as the area remains in the warm sector. Shower chances \nwill diminish on Wednesday as the cold front moves through bringing \ncooler, drier air back into the U.P. Probabilities have increased \nfor 1\"+ rainfall (in 24 hours) to the 25-50% range, highest near the \nMI/WI border. For 0.5\" or more probabilities are closer to 60-75%. \nThus it is looking likely to see a beneficial rainfall event over \nthe area. Along with the rainfall temperatures remain unseasonably \nwarm through Tuesday with highs in the low 60s (15-20F above average \nfor early November). It does cool off behind the front with highs in \nthe 50s Wednesday and Thursday, though this is still 5-10F above \naverage.",
